1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,332
Welcome to this IETF 127 Hackathon tutorial.

2
00:00:04,372 --> 00:00:10,665
In the next few minutes, you will learn
how to use Wireshark 4.6.9 and our Lua

3
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:17,404
dissector to see CoAP messages travelling
inside Bundle Protocol version 7 bundles.

4
00:00:17,944 --> 00:00:23,375
This work is championed onsite by
Professor Anna Calveras, and remotely by

5
00:00:23,415 --> 00:00:27,663
Mahitab S. Elattar, of the
Wireless Networks Group at UPC.

6
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:31,125
First, one important idea.

7
00:00:31,165 --> 00:00:37,941
The experiment itself is implemented by
modified aiocoap applications on the Rover

8
00:00:37,981 --> 00:00:43,344
and on Earth, talking through AAP2
to patched µD3TN bundle nodes.

9
00:00:43,884 --> 00:00:45,661
Wireshark only observes.

10
00:00:45,701 --> 00:00:52,776
tcpdump or dumpcap captures the packets, and our
Lua dissector adds CoAP field-level decoding.

11
00:00:52,816 --> 00:00:54,138
It is a dissector:

12
00:00:54,178 --> 00:00:57,696
not a transport, not a
proxy, and not a router.

13
00:00:58,236 --> 00:01:01,247
The question we answer together is simple:

14
00:01:01,287 --> 00:01:07,204
can one BPv7 bundle carry two non-confirmable
POST requests, and still deliver two

15
00:01:07,244 --> 00:01:13,235
correctly matched responses? To find out,
we inspect the real frames, endpoint IDs,

16
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:15,632
lifetime, message IDs and tokens.

17
00:01:16,972 --> 00:01:18,816
Here is the testbed.

18
00:01:18,856 --> 00:01:26,538
The Rover, at 192.168.100.3, runs the
Safety client on service 10, and the Science

19
00:01:26,578 --> 00:01:28,516
client on service 11.

20
00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:34,686
The Satellite, at 192.168.100.2,
is only a BPv7 relay.

21
00:01:34,726 --> 00:01:39,516
It forwards bundles, and it has no CoAP proxy.

22
00:01:40,056 --> 00:01:47,221
Earth, at 192.168.100.1, runs
the CoAP server on service 64.

23
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:56,023
We capture on Earth, on interface enp0s8,
where the Satellite delivers bundles over

24
00:01:56,063 --> 00:01:59,024
TCPCL version 3, on port 4773.

25
00:02:00,364 --> 00:02:08,151
Before you filter anything, check which
configuration your lab is running.

26
00:02:08,191 --> 00:02:17,036
The recorded experiment uses allocator
974848, with TCPCL ports 4771, 4772 and 4773.

27
00:02:17,576 --> 00:02:26,553
Older repository documentation describes a
baseline with allocator 0, and ports 4671 to 4673.

28
00:02:27,093 --> 00:02:34,715
So always check the running µD3TN node ID and CLA
settings, and use the values from your own lab.

29
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:36,768
Please do not mix the two.

30
00:02:38,108 --> 00:02:50,047
Why Wireshark 4.6.9? Because its native BPv7
decoder shows the RFC 9758 endpoint fields directly.

31
00:02:50,087 --> 00:02:55,481
Take Earth's CoAP endpoint, ipn:974848.3.64.

32
00:02:56,021 --> 00:03:00,455
Scheme code 2 tells you that this
endpoint uses the IPN scheme.

33
00:03:00,995 --> 00:03:06,874
An IPN element count of 3 tells you the
address is encoded as three separate parts:

34
00:03:06,914 --> 00:03:08,942
allocator, node, and service.

35
00:03:09,482 --> 00:03:16,967
Here, the allocator is 974848, the node is 3,
and the service is 64, Earth's CoAP application.

36
00:03:17,007 --> 00:03:17,687
Remember:

37
00:03:17,727 --> 00:03:22,091
scheme code and element count
are two different fields.

38
00:03:23,431 --> 00:03:27,205
You do not need to write a new dissector.

39
00:03:27,245 --> 00:03:34,926
Clone the project repository,
ENTEL-WNG/CoAPoverBP-IPN-Draft05-NoProxy, or use your

40
00:03:34,966 --> 00:03:36,601
existing checkout.

41
00:03:37,141 --> 00:03:41,779
The experimental Lua file
is in the wireshark folder.

42
00:03:41,819 --> 00:03:46,986
It decodes the BPv7 payload
for IPN services 10, 11 and 64.

43
00:03:48,326 --> 00:03:51,892
Next, find where your Wireshark
looks for Lua plugins.

44
00:03:51,932 --> 00:03:56,968
Open Help, About Wireshark, then Folders,
and look for Personal Lua Plugins.

45
00:03:57,508 --> 00:04:01,647
Create that folder if it is missing,
and copy the Lua file into it.

46
00:04:01,687 --> 00:04:05,765
Always use the exact folder that
your running Wireshark shows you.

47
00:04:06,305 --> 00:04:12,887
On a native Linux install, this is
usually ~/.local/lib/wireshark/plugins.

48
00:04:12,927 --> 00:04:16,914
The Flatpak version may use a different path.

49
00:04:16,954 --> 00:04:18,973
Then restart Wireshark.

50
00:04:20,313 --> 00:04:25,869
To check that the dissector loaded, open a
bundle capture or start live traffic, and

51
00:04:25,909 --> 00:04:27,601
try these display filters.

52
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:31,132
They go in Wireshark's filter
bar, not in a terminal.

53
00:04:31,672 --> 00:04:34,337
bpv7 shows all bundles.

54
00:04:34,377 --> 00:04:45,510
coapbp05.count == 1 shows Single bundles, and
coapbp05.count == 2 shows a two-member Aggregate.

55
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:52,332
bpv7.eid.ipn_count == 3 shows
three-element IPN addresses.

56
00:04:52,872 --> 00:04:58,848
In the packet details, you should see DTN
Bundle Protocol Version 7, then CoAP over

57
00:04:58,888 --> 00:05:00,878
BPv7, then each CoAP member.

58
00:05:00,918 --> 00:05:08,055
If the count filter is not recognized, check the
plugin folder, and look for Lua errors at startup.

59
00:05:09,395 --> 00:05:12,757
There are two supported ways to capture.

60
00:05:12,797 --> 00:05:19,562
With native Wireshark, simply select
enp0s8 in the interface list, and start the

61
00:05:19,602 --> 00:05:22,710
capture before the new TCPCL traffic.

62
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:27,048
Optionally, use a capture
filter for TCP port 4773.

63
00:05:27,588 --> 00:05:34,014
With the Wireshark 4.6.9 Flatpak, the
sandbox cannot capture enp0s8 directly.

64
00:05:34,054 --> 00:05:40,397
Instead, host tcpdump sends the packets
through a named pipe into Wireshark.

65
00:05:40,437 --> 00:05:43,588
These are still genuine, live packets.

66
00:05:44,128 --> 00:05:48,759
The named-pipe workaround is the one
used in the verified Earth demonstration.

67
00:05:50,099 --> 00:05:52,225
Here is how that pipe works.

68
00:05:52,265 --> 00:05:56,634
The packets stay on the real
interface, enp0s8, on Earth.

69
00:05:57,174 --> 00:06:03,030
Host tcpdump reads the live frames, and
streams them, unbuffered, through a named pipe.

70
00:06:03,570 --> 00:06:10,549
The sandboxed Wireshark reads the pipe,
and decodes TCPCL version 3, BPv7, and CoAP

71
00:06:10,589 --> 00:06:12,240
with the Lua plugin.

72
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:19,350
On Earth, run the helper script from the
scripts folder, with the interface name.

73
00:06:19,390 --> 00:06:22,695
Keep its terminal open while you capture;

74
00:06:22,735 --> 00:06:26,367
it does not restart µD3TN or any application.

75
00:06:26,407 --> 00:06:32,732
To capture only the Earth TCPCL port,
set the capture filter to tcp port 4773.

76
00:06:34,072 --> 00:06:37,504
Now, find your TCPCL conversation.

77
00:06:37,544 --> 00:06:45,878
At first, packets may appear as plain TCP.
Use the display filter tcp.port == 4773

78
00:06:45,918 --> 00:06:53,230
for the current run, or 4673 for the
older baseline, together with bpv7.

79
00:06:53,770 --> 00:07:02,382
If the packets still remain TCP, use Analyze,
Decode As, and select TCPCL version 3 for port 4773.

80
00:07:02,422 --> 00:07:10,681
And start the capture before the TCPCL handshake
when you can, because TCP reassembly matters.

81
00:07:11,221 --> 00:07:12,055
One more tip:

82
00:07:12,095 --> 00:07:17,031
a single Ethernet frame's length is
not the full bundle or payload length.

83
00:07:17,071 --> 00:07:19,385
Always read the reassembled values.

84
00:07:20,725 --> 00:07:22,523
Let's look at real results.

85
00:07:22,563 --> 00:07:25,926
Frame 14 is the Single
request, captured on Earth.

86
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:37,523
It travels from the Rover Safety service,
ipn:974848.1.10, to Earth's CoAP service,

87
00:07:37,563 --> 00:07:42,603
ipn:974848.3.64, over TCPCL version 3.

88
00:07:43,143 --> 00:07:50,308
Inside is exactly one non-confirmable GET,
with its 24-bit message ID and token, in

89
00:07:50,348 --> 00:07:52,131
a 59-byte BP payload.

90
00:07:52,671 --> 00:07:56,309
Because it is a Single message,
there is no Payload-length option.

91
00:07:57,649 --> 00:08:11,074
Earth replies in a separate bundle, frame 31,
from ipn:974848.3.64 back to ipn:974848.1.10.

92
00:08:11,614 --> 00:08:17,676
It is a non-confirmable 2.05 Content
response, in a 103-byte payload.

93
00:08:18,216 --> 00:08:24,395
And it carries the request's token,
684d4727, so the Rover can match it.

94
00:08:25,735 --> 00:08:27,345
Now, the Aggregate test.

95
00:08:27,385 --> 00:08:32,569
In this controlled experiment, NSTART
is set to two, the buffer limit is two

96
00:08:32,609 --> 00:08:36,074
messages, and the aggregation
timer is ten seconds.

97
00:08:36,614 --> 00:08:40,340
The Science client queues
two non-confirmable POSTs.

98
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:47,221
Each has its own 24-bit message ID and token,
and each carries 50 bytes of application payload.

99
00:08:47,761 --> 00:09:00,316
Both share the same source, ipn:974848.1.11,
and the same destination, ipn:974848.3.64.

100
00:09:01,656 --> 00:09:03,765
Frame 76 shows the result.

101
00:09:04,305 --> 00:09:16,996
One BPv7 bundle travels from
ipn:974848.1.11 to ipn:974848.3.64.

102
00:09:17,536 --> 00:09:22,665
Its 212-byte application payload
contains two CoAP POST messages:

103
00:09:22,705 --> 00:09:26,244
one bundle, not two separate request bundles.

104
00:09:27,584 --> 00:09:32,384
The Lua dissector expands each member
independently. Here is member one:

105
00:09:32,424 --> 00:09:33,930
a non-confirmable POST.

106
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:41,421
You can read its 24-bit message ID,
2543436, and its token, 2f8ecb82.

107
00:09:41,461 --> 00:09:47,803
Member two follows, with the next
message ID and its own token.

108
00:09:48,343 --> 00:09:54,967
Each member also shows its URI options and the
experimental Payload-length option: 50 bytes.

109
00:09:56,307 --> 00:10:01,628
Wireshark 4.6.9 confirms the
addressing for both endpoints.

110
00:10:01,668 --> 00:10:03,809
Scheme code 2 means IPN.

111
00:10:04,349 --> 00:10:06,414
The IPN element count is 3.

112
00:10:06,954 --> 00:10:09,185
The allocator is 974848.

113
00:10:09,225 --> 00:10:16,374
The source is node 1, service 11, and
the destination is node 3, service 64.

114
00:10:17,714 --> 00:10:25,070
Next, verify the bundle's lifetime and integrity
in the BPv7 primary block. The version is 7.

115
00:10:25,610 --> 00:10:29,943
The lifetime is 90,000
milliseconds, that is, 90 seconds.

116
00:10:30,483 --> 00:10:35,456
The CRC type is CRC-16,
and the CRC status is Good.

117
00:10:35,996 --> 00:10:41,069
Remember to read these values from the primary
block, not from the Ethernet frame length.

118
00:10:42,409 --> 00:10:44,382
Finally, the application view.

119
00:10:44,422 --> 00:10:48,876
Rover Mission Control reports four
of four matching Safety replies,

120
00:10:49,416 --> 00:10:51,707
and three of three matching Science replies.

121
00:10:52,247 --> 00:10:57,998
The two Aggregate POST members received
separate 2.01 Created responses, each with

122
00:10:58,038 --> 00:10:59,340
its matching token.

123
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:04,168
When you present your
results, be precise. Verified:

124
00:11:04,208 --> 00:11:10,004
Single and Aggregate framing, three-element
endpoint IDs, and token-matched responses.

125
00:11:10,544 --> 00:11:11,451
Experimental:

126
00:11:11,491 --> 00:11:17,352
Payload-length option number 65000 is not
a final assigned number, so inspect its

127
00:11:17,392 --> 00:11:20,922
option properties before
making standards claims.

128
00:11:21,462 --> 00:11:22,221
Congestion:

129
00:11:22,261 --> 00:11:28,178
NSTART of two was used only in a controlled
test, so congestion safety still needs

130
00:11:28,218 --> 00:11:30,285
deployment-specific analysis.

131
00:11:30,825 --> 00:11:31,494
And scope:

132
00:11:31,534 --> 00:11:33,689
the Lua plugin decodes traffic.

133
00:11:33,729 --> 00:11:37,089
It does not implement
CoAP, routing, or a proxy.

134
00:11:37,129 --> 00:11:43,463
This is a working prototype, not proof of full
draft-five conformance or interoperability.

135
00:11:44,803 --> 00:11:48,528
To share a reproducible run,
follow these six steps. One:

136
00:11:48,568 --> 00:11:52,954
install the matching Lua plugin,
in the folder Wireshark shows you.

137
00:11:53,494 --> 00:11:53,769
Two:

138
00:11:53,809 --> 00:12:00,377
start the capture before the test, using
native enp0s8 or the Flatpak pipe launcher.

139
00:12:00,917 --> 00:12:03,901
Three: confirm the decoding:

140
00:12:03,941 --> 00:12:09,084
TCPCL version 3, then
BPv7, then CoAP over BPv7.

141
00:12:09,624 --> 00:12:09,952
Four:

142
00:12:09,992 --> 00:12:11,572
record the IPN fields:

143
00:12:11,612 --> 00:12:14,737
element count, allocator, node and service.

144
00:12:15,277 --> 00:12:15,635
Five:

145
00:12:15,675 --> 00:12:18,102
record the transaction details:

146
00:12:18,142 --> 00:12:22,639
message IDs, tokens, payload
sizes, lifetime and replies.

147
00:12:23,179 --> 00:12:23,735
And six:

148
00:12:23,775 --> 00:12:30,288
save the pcapng file and the logs, without
secrets, credentials, or private local files.

149
00:12:31,628 --> 00:12:34,477
Everything you need is in the repository:

150
00:12:34,517 --> 00:12:39,830
the experimental Lua dissector, the
code, captures, and setup documentation.

151
00:12:40,370 --> 00:12:44,253
Thank you for following this
tutorial. Onsite Champion:

152
00:12:44,293 --> 00:12:50,101
Professor Anna Calveras. Remote Champion:
Mahitab S. Elattar. Enjoy the Hackathon!
